WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.935
-Our next guest is
an Emmy Award-winning actor

00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:04.437
you know from his work
on "Boardwalk Empire"

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.739
and films like "The Irishman,"
"Ant-Man," and "Blonde."

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:07.907
He stars in "The Watcher,"

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.609
which is streaming now
on Netflix.

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.310
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.780
-"657 Boulevard has been
the subject of my family

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.181
for decades now.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.783
And as it approaches
its 100th birthday,

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.250
I've been put in charge
of watching

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:20.520
and waiting
for its second coming.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.189
Someone watched the house
in the 1920s,

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.925
and someone else
watched in the 1960s.

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:27.626
It is now my time.

00:00:27.626 --> 00:00:29.328
Do you know
the history of the house?

00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:33.733
Do you know what lies within
the walls of 657 Boulevard?"

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:36.502
-"I wonder
what brought you here.

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:39.572
I see already that you have
flooded 657 Boulevard

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:43.108
with contractors so that
you can finish the basement.

00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:45.311
Why was it left that way?

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:48.715
It might not frighten you yet,
but it will."

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:50.416
-Please welcome back
to the show our friend

00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:52.251
Bobby Cannavale, everybody.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:53.986
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:53.986 --> 00:01:02.228 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:10.469 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:11.804
Welcome back.
-Hi. Thank you.

00:01:11.804 --> 00:01:13.739
-I'm so happy to have you.
-Thank you for that.

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:15.174
-We both have three kids.
-Yeah.

00:01:15.174 --> 00:01:17.343
-We've got a couple that are --
are very similar ages.

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:18.811
You've got
a 6- and a 5-year-old.

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:21.080
I've got a 6 and a 4-year-old.
I also have a 1-year-old.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.782
You also have a 27-year-old.
-I do.

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:25.517
I -- I've mostly been
parenting my whole life.

00:01:25.517 --> 00:01:27.152
-Yeah.
-Most of my life. Yeah.

00:01:27.152 --> 00:01:29.555
-Is the -- I mean, it obviously
is a big age gap,

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:31.524
and I'm wondering --
You just did a movie

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:33.993
with the incredibly funny
Bill Burr called "Old Dads."

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.494
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:37.730
-So, this is about people
who have kids later in life.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.365
I feel like
I had kids later in life,

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.234
but you also had one
early in life.

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.202
Have you noticed the difference?

00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:45.104
-Oh, yeah, big-time.

00:01:45.104 --> 00:01:47.206
I mean, I -- I can't -- We were
at the playground yesterday,

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:49.542
and I -- and my --
Both guys were like,

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.945
"Dad, you get on the --
on the monkey bars."

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:55.348
And I was like -- I tried.

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:57.116
And I just --
I couldn't do it.

00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:58.718
My shoulders just --
They didn't --

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:00.553
They just wouldn't do it.
I couldn't do it.

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:01.988
-Yeah.
-And I think I can do it.

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.589
There's many things
you think you can do.

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:05.291
But, you know, the main
difference is that, you know,

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:07.260
Jake was pretty much
an only child growing up,

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.128
and now I have
these two animals, you know,

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:11.096
just all over each other
all the time.

00:02:11.096 --> 00:02:13.332
-Do you ever ask Jake
to speak on your behalf

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:15.301
and tell the younger ones
that there was a time

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:17.303
where you were an active father?

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:19.305
He's like,
"Oh, you should have seen him

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:20.806
on the monkey bars in the '90s!"

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:22.174
-I have. I have. Alright.

00:02:22.174 --> 00:02:24.110
Yeah, and as Jake
pointed out to me, he's like,

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.211
"You know, dad, it's good
that you had these kids later
and that you had me earlier

00:02:26.211 --> 00:02:30.182
so that I could then tell them
all about you later."

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:31.951
I'm like,
"I'm not going anywhere."

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:34.687
You know? But it's the
5-year-old's birthday today.

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:37.189
-Oh, and by the way --
So 5-year-old birthday.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:40.226
Is it true that the older one
is not enthusiastic

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.894
about the fact
that you're celebrating?

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:43.295
-He's not. He's not.

00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:45.398
He's -- He's advocating
to just ignore the birthday.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:47.267
He's like -- He's like,
"I just don't understand."

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:49.602
First of all, he's using -- I
don't know if your kids do this,

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.905
but, like, Rocco's very into
using words incorrectly.

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:53.606
Like, new words that he likes.
-Yeah.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.608
-And lately, he loves the word
"immediately."

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.276
So he's like, "I don't
immediately understand

00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:59.112
why we have to
celebrate his birthday."

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:01.480
I'm like, "That's not how you
use the word 'immediately.'"

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.016
And he's like,
"I don't understand.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.316
We have my birthday in February.

00:03:04.316 --> 00:03:06.219
Why do we have to celebrate
his in November?

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:07.820
I don't immediately understand."

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.822
And I'm like -- So --
-Give yourself time.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.791
You don't have to
understand immediately.

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:13.826
-Yeah, yeah.
-Give yourself a couple hours.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:16.162
Family photo right there.
There's all three kids.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:17.663
-Yeah. There we go.

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:20.800
And your wonderful
Hulk costume there.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.268
You really --
You put the effort in.

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:24.169
-And then your beautiful wife,
Rose Byrne, there.

00:03:24.169 --> 00:03:25.804
-Solved the answ--
I solved the question,

00:03:25.804 --> 00:03:27.006
"Where does Hulk
put his wallet?"

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:28.274
-Yeah. There you go.

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:31.110
-Got a little bag there.
-Yeah.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.179
And I think
that's why he was mad.

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.548
-That's right.
-Bruce Banner had to wear

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:38.384
a fanny pack, and then
he was so angry about it...

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.852
-That's right.
-...he turned into the Hulk.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:41.254
-That's right.
That was classic, though.

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:42.688
I wasn't planning on that --
wearing that.

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:44.590
Rose was like, "Well, who's
gonna carry the keys?"

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:47.793
And I was like, "I guess
I'll put the fanny pack on."

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:50.129
-I know that you are -- you're
a pretty good chef, right?

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:52.064
You like to cook.
-I do. I like to cook. Yeah.

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:53.632
-And do the kids
like your cooking?

00:03:53.632 --> 00:03:55.868
-I mean, I'm the only one
in the house that does cook.

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:57.470
-Okay, gotcha.
-The kids got to eat.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.705
-They do got to eat. Right.

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:00.440
At some point,
you got to learn a skill.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.841
-Yeah.
-Do they like your food?

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:03.676
Do you like --
-They do. They like my food.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:06.879
You know, the problem is that
they love bread, you know?

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.347
-They just love bread, man.

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:12.051
So I'm constantly
figuring out ways to just make

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:15.255
bread an interesting
new way to eat bread.

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:17.256
-That is a real challenge
for a chef, yeah.

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:19.558
-Yeah, it is. So, you know,
today was French toast.

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:21.027
We got, you know,
Vegemite toast.

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:23.829
We have, you know,
bread with cheese on it,

00:04:23.829 --> 00:04:25.698
you know, grilled cheese.

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.167
But they like my -- You know,

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:32.638
I make a pretty good
tomato sauce and...

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.740
you know, burgers and --

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.709
You know, they're kids, man.
-Yeah.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:38.211
-So it's tough to find things

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:39.879
that they like,
particularly at lunchtime.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:42.081
You know, they go to a school
where you have to make lunch,

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.617
and I'm just stumped
all the time.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:45.785
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:47.152
-Nothing is worse
than when they bring home --

00:04:47.152 --> 00:04:49.121
I don't know if you've had this,
where they bring home

00:04:49.121 --> 00:04:51.557
the lunchbox,
and you just open it up

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:54.527
praying that it won't be
exactly as you packed it.

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:56.628
-Yes. That's exactly right.
-You're like, "Just one bite."

00:04:56.628 --> 00:04:58.831
-It's the first question I ask.
It's the first question I ask.

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:00.399
"Did you eat the lunch?"
"I took a bite."

00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:03.669
And, like, everything's gone.
Like, the Pirate Booty is gone.

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:06.038
The little Bunny Grahams
are gone.

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:07.506
-Yeah.
-And it's just carrots

00:05:07.506 --> 00:05:08.841
and ham sandwich.

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:11.010
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.445
-Now, this show
we saw a clip from,

00:05:13.445 --> 00:05:14.846
it's based on a true story.
-Yeah.

00:05:14.846 --> 00:05:18.350
-It's a very haunting true story
about a suburban family

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.119
who started receiving letters
like that.

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:21.787
Their house is being watched.

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:23.489
Were you aware of it at all
before you took it on?

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:26.758
-You know, I mean, I was just
in terms of being turned --

00:05:26.758 --> 00:05:28.694
being given the story,
the "New York" article,

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:30.128
which was very, very popular.

00:05:30.128 --> 00:05:32.998
You know, before the show,
there was this one source,

00:05:32.998 --> 00:05:34.867
this "New York Magazine" story

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.702
that was, like,
viewed so many times.

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:38.438
-Yeah, everybody
passed it around.

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:39.805
-Everybody passed it around.

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:42.542
So clearly it was resonating
for people on some level.

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:45.010
And I think, you know,
Ryan Murphy, who's sort of

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.246
very good at having his finger
on the zeitgeist, just thought,

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:49.949
"You know, that would be a good
idea to make a show about it."

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:51.984
But how he initially
approached me about it,

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:53.719
you know, it was during COVID,
and he was like,

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:55.621
"I don't know about you, man,
but COVID made me feel

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:59.258
really powerless to be able
to protect my family."

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.961
And I think for him, it was more
of an allegorical thing,

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:04.063
you know, to tell the story
in the guise of, like,

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:05.965
it's about a house
and getting these letters,

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:08.200
but really it's about fear
and it's about people

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.470
being unable to control
what they can't control.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.471
And I think that's what
people respond to.

00:06:12.471 --> 00:06:14.606
It's why, you know, we have
such a big reaction to,

00:06:14.606 --> 00:06:16.375
like, the ending,
which I won't give away.

00:06:16.375 --> 00:06:18.744
But, you know, I have people
stopping me in the street now,

00:06:18.744 --> 00:06:20.613
you know,
giving me their opinion

00:06:20.613 --> 00:06:22.047
about the ending, you know?

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:23.949
-You were saying --
I mean, this is --

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:25.651
I mean,
this has been so popular.

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:27.419
And I think you're an actor

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:29.154
that everybody knows you
from a different thing,

00:06:29.154 --> 00:06:30.456
but this last month
must be crazy

00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:31.924
because I would imagine
almost everyone

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.459
who is approaching you
is approaching you

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:35.395
about this one specific --
-About the one thing, yeah.

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:37.763
Usually, it's like --
Yeah, it's --

00:06:37.763 --> 00:06:39.398
I can't --
It's different things.

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:41.800
Everything from, "Did we go
to high school together?"

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.803
To, you know --

00:06:44.803 --> 00:06:46.505
Yeah, you know,
"Third Watch," "Will &amp; Grace."

00:06:46.505 --> 00:06:49.207
There's all these
different things.

00:06:49.207 --> 00:06:52.211
So this has been pretty --
It's been pretty --

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:54.747
Yeah, it's obvious
how popular this thing is.

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:57.450
-So popular that a second season
was just announced.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.151
-Just today.
-Congratulations.

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:00.887
-Thank you.

00:07:00.887 --> 00:07:02.521
-Everybody is
going to have more things

00:07:02.521 --> 00:07:04.056
to talk to you
on the street about.

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:06.025
-The Watcher is still watching.
-Still watching.

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:07.559
-Hey. Thanks so much
for being here.

00:07:07.559 --> 00:07:09.061
It's always a pleasure
to see you.

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:11.197
Love to you and the family.
Bobby Cannavale.

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:13.232
"The Watcher" is streaming now
on Netflix.

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:15.368
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

